1: # Independent audit of the three source-zero prism fans
2: 
3: This is a separate working audit of the explicit local cone collections
4: in `notes/part3_source_zero_prism_working.md`. It does not promote a
5: global K39 filling or assign a source-loop return. The calculation uses
6: only the current explicit matrices, rays, and staircase tetrahedra.
7: 
8: The degree/support argument passes. In addition, the noncollapsed
9: cones, together with all their faces, already form a fan in each of
10: the three sectors. The collapsed cones in either rank-one sector are
11: existing faces of the noncollapsed cones. A periodic common refinement
12: of the three H-boundary fans, with compatible rational integral-sector
13: refinements, is constructed below. The unit action, quotient properness,
14: and analytic cap attachment remain separate steps.
15: 
16: ## PF.1. Exact domain, slice map, and the degree argument
17: 
18: Keep the actual integer pairs \((a,b)=(1,2),(2,1)\), the integer
19: \(N>1\), and
20: \[
21:  I=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
22:  W=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix},\quad
23:  E=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&N\end{pmatrix},\quad
24:  H=\begin{pmatrix}2&b\\-a&N-1\end{pmatrix}.
25: \]
26: For each pair \((P_0,H)\), the original rays and action are
27: \[
28:  R_0(n)=(P_0n,1,0),\quad R_1(n)=(Hn,0,1),\quad
29:  T_m(x,r,s)=(x+rP_0m+sHm,r,s).                         \tag{PF.1}
30: \]
31: All geometric inspection changes below leave these integral vectors
32: unchanged; they are explicitly rational linear maps used to inspect
33: their cones.
34: 
35: Start with the same staircase cones for \(P_0=P_1=I\). On
36: \(r+s=1\) they are exactly the standard triangulation of
37: \(\mathbb R^2\times[0,1]\). The two unit-square triangles
38: have ordered vertices \((0,u,u+v)\), \((0,v,u+v)\), and
39: the three tetrahedra on each are those in the root note. The common
40: diagonal of every vertical rectangle agrees on adjacent prisms, so
41: this is a face-to-face domain triangulation.
42: 
43: Linearly map its vertices to (PF.1). At height \(s=t\), the
44: map is \(F_t\), with literal periodicity
45: \[
46:  F_t(x+m)=F_t(x)+P(t)m,\qquad P(t)=(1-t)P_0+tH.          \tag{PF.2}
47: \]
48: Its difference from \(P(t)x\) is periodic and bounded. The
49: three determinants, at arbitrary positive \(r,s\), are
50: \[
51:  \begin{array}{ll}
52:  P_0=I:&r^2+(N+1)rs+2Ns^2,\\
53:  P_0=W:&(N-1)rs+2Ns^2,\\
54:  P_0=E:&2Nrs+2Ns^2.
55:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.3}
56: \]
57: They are positive. Hence (PF.2) descends to the stated two-torus
58: map and the straight-line periodic homotopy gives degree one.
59: On every noncollapsed slice piece its Jacobian is the signed
60: four-dimensional determinant ratio. These are exactly
61: \[
62:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
63:   & (0,u,u+v)&(0,v,u+v)\\ \hline
64:  (I,H)&(1,N-1,2N)&(1,2,2N)\\
65:  (W,H)&(0,N-1,2N)&(0,0,2N)\\
66:  (E,H)&(0,0,2N)&(0,2N,2N).
67:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.4}
68: \]
69: Here each determinant is divided by the determinant of the same
70: domain cone, so its orientation sign is fixed rather than inferred
71: from an absolute determinant.
72: 
73: Outside the finite polygonal images of lower-dimensional and collapsed
74: pieces on the quotient torus, every preimage lies in a noncollapsed
75: piece and has positive local degree. Degree one therefore gives one
76: preimage. It follows that distinct noncollapsed image interiors do
77: not overlap. Their closures cover each slice: their finite union on
78: the quotient is closed and contains the dense set of these regular
79: values. Lifting and using the bounded periodic difference proves full
80: support in the whole plane. This verifies exactly the root note's
81: degree/support statement. It does not claim raw injectivity in a
82: rank-one sector.
83: 
84: ## PF.2. The I,H sector has exact common faces
85: 
86: Every entry in its determinant row is strictly positive. Triangulate
87: the slice parallelograms by a diagonal, without changing their affine
88: maps. In the resulting planar triangulation, an edge cannot fold:
89: the two positively oriented adjacent triangles map to opposite sides
90: of its image. At a vertex, the images of successive positively
91: oriented sectors turn through strictly positive angles less than
92: \(\pi\). Their closed link has positive integral winding, so
93: the local degree is a positive integer. The same is true in an edge
94: or face interior.
95: 
96: The global degree is one. The sum of these positive local degrees
97: over any fibre is consequently one, including values on the images
98: of edges and vertices. Equivalently one can take a small regular
99: value in the link disc. Thus every point has exactly one preimage
100: and local degree one. Properness follows from (PF.2); the slice map
101: is a homeomorphism. The endpoint slice maps are the invertible
102: linear maps \(I\) and \(H\), and the same conclusion holds
103: there.
104: 
105: The full map preserves the two heights and is homogeneous. It is
106: therefore injective on the union of the original cones. For two
107: domain cones \(C,D\),
108: \[
109:                     F(C)\cap F(D)=F(C\cap D).            \tag{PF.5}
110: \]
111: Their original intersection is a common face, and each cone map is
112: linear and invertible. Thus (PF.5) is a common face of the two image
113: cones. This proves the fan property for the I,H sector without an
114: additional refinement.
115: 
116: ## PF.3. A literal strip model for both rank-one sectors
117: 
118: For W use the prescribed basis
119: \(u=e_1,v=e_2-be_1\). Then \(P_0v=0\). For E exchange the
120: two basis vectors, so \(u=e_2,v=e_1\), and again \(P_0v=0\).
121: Exchanging these vectors only exchanges the two triangles in the
122: given collection; it does not change its cones. Put
123: \[
124:                    p=P_0u,\qquad A=Hu,\qquad B=Hv.
125: \]
126: Apply the rational inspection map with basis \((A,B)\) in the
127: two fibre coordinates. It sends \(A\) to \((1,0)\),
128: \(B\) to \((0,1)\), and \(p\) to \((c,d)\), where
129: \[
130:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
131:        &c&d\\ \hline
132:  W&(N-1)/(2N)&-a/(2N)\\
133:  E&1&-b/2.
134:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.6}
135: \]
136: In each case \(c>0\). This map is not declared unimodular;
137: all actual rays remain (PF.1) in their original lattice.
138: 
139: At fixed \(r,s>0\), the two top-triangle cones give precisely
140: the square
141: \[
142:                 Q=[0,s](1,0)+[0,s](0,1),
143: \]
144: split along its diagonal from \((0,0)\) to \((s,s)\).
145: The one noncollapsed mixed cone gives the parallelogram
146: \[
147:            J=(s,0)+[0,r](c,d)+[0,s](0,1).                \tag{PF.7}
148: \]
149: These are the three noncollapsed maximal slice cells of the original
150: prism collection, not a replacement collection. Their union is
151: exactly
152: \[
153:  \begin{split}
154:  0\le x\le L:=s+rc,&\qquad f(x)\le y\le f(x)+s,\\
155:  f(x)&=\begin{cases}
156:        0,&0\le x\le s,\\
157:        (d/c)(x-s),&s\le x\le s+rc.
158:        \end{cases}
159:  \end{split}                                             \tag{PF.8}
160: \]
161: The actual periods in these coordinates are
162: \[
163:                  rP_0u+sHu=(L,rd),\qquad
164:                  rP_0v+sHv=(0,s).                        \tag{PF.9}
165: \]
166: They are exactly the translations used to tile by (PF.8).
167: 
168: Here is an explicit inverse cell assignment. For a point off the
169: displayed boundaries, choose the unique integer \(i\) with
170: \(0<x-iL<L\), then the unique integer \(j\) with
171: \[
172:   f(x-iL)<y-i rd-js<f(x-iL)+s.
173: \]
174: The point belongs to the cell labelled \(iu+jv\). Within that
175: cell, \(x-iL<s\) places it in Q, where the single diagonal
176: chooses one of the two triangles. For \(x-iL>s\), its actual
177: parallelogram coordinates are
178: \[
179:  \alpha=\frac{x-iL-s}{rc},\qquad
180:  \beta=\frac{y-i rd-js-(d/c)(x-iL-s)}s,
181:                  \qquad 0<\alpha,\beta<1.                \tag{PF.10}
182: \]
183: This proves both uniqueness and coverage with the exact period maps.
184: 
185: All boundary identifications in this tiling are whole faces. The
186: square and J share their full vertical edge. The right edge of J
187: is the left edge of the next square, under \((L,rd)\).
188: The top piecewise-linear boundary is the bottom boundary translated
189: by \((0,s)\), and the break at \(x=s\) occurs at exactly the
190: same place on both sides. The square's diagonal meets these edges
191: only at its two endpoints. Thus no T-junction or partial-edge
192: intersection remains. This explicitly proves common faces for all
193: positive height ratios, not merely disjoint interiors.
194: 
195: The ray identifications are independent of \(r,s\). Writing
196: the cell label as zero, the two collapsed bottom tetrahedra both
197: have the image face
198: \[
199:        \operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_0(u),R_1(u+v)),        \tag{PF.11}
200: \]
201: because \(R_0(v)=R_0(0)\) and
202: \(R_0(u+v)=R_0(u)\). This is the upper face of J and the
203: lower face of its v-translate. The other collapsed mixed tetrahedron
204: has the image face
205: \[
206:        \operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_1(v),R_1(u+v)),        \tag{PF.12}
207: \]
208: the top edge of Q and the bottom edge of its v-translate. Equations
209: (PF.11)--(PF.12) show that omitting the collapsed maximal cones and
210: then adjoining the ordinary faces loses no ray or required face.
211: 
212: The remaining face incidences are also literal common-ray subsets:
213: the diagonal of Q uses \(R_0(0),R_1(0),R_1(u+v)\);
214: the Q/J edge uses \(R_0(0),R_1(u),R_1(u+v)\);
215: the J/next-Q edge uses \(R_0(u),R_1(u),R_1(u+v)\).
216: Together with their period translates they give every interior
217: facet. The rank-one base face has just the rays \(R_0(iu)\)
218: and intervals between consecutive such rays; the other base face
219: has the ordinary H-triangle fan. These are faces of the same cones.
220: This completes the common-face proof, including the height boundary.
221: 
222: ## PF.4. A compatible periodic refinement on the common H boundary
223: 
224: Apply \(H^{-1}\) on the H-height-one slice, solely to describe
225: its geometry. The I and E sectors give the standard unit-square
226: triangulation \(\mathcal T\); W gives its integral shear
227: \(\mathcal T_b\), with basis \(e_1,e_2-be_1\). Both are
228: \(\mathbb Z^2\)-periodic, locally finite, rational triangulations.
229: Take their literal overlay
230: \[
231:        \mathcal P=\{\sigma\cap\tau:
232:                    \sigma\in\mathcal T,\ 
233:                    \tau\in\mathcal T_b\},              \tag{PF.13}
234: \]
235: including all its faces and omitting empty intersections. This is
236: an exact common polyhedral refinement. Indeed a face of a member
237: is obtained by an active supporting inequality from one of its
238: two factors; intersecting two members activates the corresponding
239: common faces in the two original complexes. Their intersection is
240: therefore a common face. Local finiteness follows because a compact
241: set meets finitely many triangles in each factor. The periodic
242: quotient has finitely many cells, and every vertex is rational.
243: 
244: The certificate computes this overlay with rational arithmetic for
245: both actual b values, on a fundamental square and its neighbouring
246: period translates. It checks area one and every common-face
247: intersection. It retains the rational vertices; they are not silently
248: replaced by lattice vertices.
249: 
250: Coning the cells of \(H\mathcal P\) in the H boundary gives
251: the common rational boundary fan. Replace each rational ray by its
252: primitive integral generator in the original four-dimensional
253: lattice. This is an exact equality of the ray, not a change of the
254: lattice. If a simplicial refinement is wanted, use the barycentre
255: of each polygonal cell and the induced boundary subdivisions,
256: periodically.
257: 
258: There is an explicit compatible extension to each sector fan.
259: Prescribe that common subdivision on its H subfan, retain the other
260: base subfan, and process the remaining original faces in increasing
261: dimension. Once the boundary of a face F is subdivided, choose the
262: interior ray equal to the sum of F's primitive ray generators and
263: cone its subdivided boundary from that ray. On the slice \(r+s=1\)
264: this is the usual radial decomposition of a convex polytope from
265: an interior point: each ray from that point meets the boundary
266: once, so it covers and has common faces. Shared faces were processed
267: first and hence receive the same subdivision from both sides.
268: 
269: Every period map in (PF.1) is unimodular and carries primitive ray
270: generators to primitive ray generators. Thus the chosen sums and
271: the entire inductive construction are period-equivariant. There
272: are finitely many face orbits, so the construction has finitely
273: many subdivision types. This proves compatibility on H while
274: retaining the actual integral period actions. It does not assert
275: that the resulting cones are unimodular; a separate lattice
276: resolution may be applied to them with its own proof.
277: 
278: ## PF.5. Exact audit boundary
279: 
280: The local fan and common-H-refinement claims above pass the independent
281: geometric audit. The raw rank-one prism map still collapses whole
282: tetrahedra; no injectivity statement about that map is restored.
283: The fans are locally finite over compact subsets of the strictly
284: positive height sector and near the full-rank H face. On a rank-one
285: base face the kernel-period unit and the properness of its analytic
286: quotient remain exactly the separate issue identified in the root
287: note. This audit supplies no omitted filling point, global proper
288: family, link multiplicity, characteristic number, or return class.
289: 
290: Exact checks are saved in `code/part3/source_zero_prism_audit.py`,
291: its JSON and captured output. No earlier-run text is used.
